    "durationMs": 257672,
    "finalText": "The URL Shortener API is complete and fully verified. Here's what I built:\n\n**Stack**: NestJS 11.2.11 + TypeScript 5.8.3 with TypeORM (Nest's idiomatic persistence layer) over PostgreSQL 16, connecting via `DATABASE_URL` (nothing hard-coded).\n\n**Implementation**\n- `POST /links` — validates absolute http/https URLs and the 4–20 char `[A-Za-z0-9_-]` code rule; generates unique 8-char codes when omitted; returns `201 {code, url, clicks: 0}` with the URL unchanged; duplicates → `409`, bad input → `422`, all with an `error` field. A DB unique index backs concurrent duplicates (race test: 10 parallel POSTs → one 201, nine 409s).\n- `GET /links/{code}` → `200` or `404 {error}`; `GET /r/{code}` → `302` with `Location` and an atomic `clicks = clicks + 1` (exactly once per request); `GET /links` → `{items, total}` ordered by ascending creation.\n- Malformed JSON returns `422` via a custom body-parser middleware (Nest's default would give 400), and a global filter shapes every error as JSON.\n\n**Verification** — 37/37 curl checks passed, including a concurrent-duplicate race and a restart test: after `sh start.sh` (build → `typeorm migration:run` → serve in foreground on `0.0.0.0:8000`), links and click counts survived from the previous run. Test data was truncated afterward, the test server stopped, and the README documents startup."
